WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.710
AI workloads are often constrained by specialized resources,

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.515
not just ordinary CPU and memory.

00:00:05.515 --> 00:00:09.910
A service may be blocked by GPU availability, accelerator memory, storage throughput,

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.640
network transfer, or scheduler decisions.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.560
Resource telemetry helps teams separate model behavior problems from capacity, placement,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.720
and infrastructure problems.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.160
At the node level, teams usually need CPU, memory, disk, network, process health,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.800
file system pressure, and container or pod status.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.420
For AI workloads, accelerator signals become just as important.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.960
Useful GPU and accelerator telemetry may include utilization, memory usage, temperature,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.920
power draw, throttling, device errors, driver health, and device availability.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.995
These signals help explain why a model path may slow down even when application logs show

00:00:40.995 --> 00:00:42.400
no obvious errors.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.675
Cluster telemetry should show scheduler pressure and workload placement.

00:00:45.675 --> 00:00:50.545
Pending jobs, failed placements, evictions, queue depth, preemption, resource requests,

00:00:50.545 --> 00:00:54.195
resource limits, node labels, and capacity trends all affect AI service behavior.

00:00:54.195 --> 00:00:58.380
A model-serving endpoint may fail an SLO because requests wait behind other work,

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.595
not because the model itself changed.

00:01:00.595 --> 00:01:02.505
Storage and network telemetry also matter.

00:01:02.505 --> 00:01:06.220
Model loading, embedding indexes, checkpoints, training data, retrieval sources,

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.930
and logs can create bottlenecks.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.800
Historical telemetry supports capacity planning by showing real patterns across peak

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:12.320
traffic,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.800
batch windows, release events, and incidents.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.432
Without that history, teams often guess, overprovision,

00:01:17.432 --> 00:01:19.800
or chase symptoms instead of fixing the constrained layer.
